description: Sync must merge issue refs the way git merges branches, instead of force-overwriting them.
 
 Bug (2026-10-10, two clones of one bare remote, both with setup-sync): an unpushed local comment is dropped by a plain git fetch, which prints (forced update) for refs/notes/issue-<id>; git pull does the same through the post-merge hook. Cause: the refspec +refs/notes/*:refs/notes/* writes the remote's refs straight over the local ones, as if git fetched with +refs/heads/*:refs/heads/*. Recoverable only from the reflog.
 
 Keep per-issue refs. They exist for performance, and a single notes ref would lose all three properties: (1) no write contention, since edits to different issues touch different refs, so parallel editors do not diverge on unrelated work; (2) no process per note, since list/show read every ref in one batch (for-each-ref + cat-file --batch); (3) change detection by one sha comparison per ref, which the pre-push hook (6ff704b) and the edge index rely on.
 
 Design, after git's own branch model:
 1. Fetch into a tracking namespace: +refs/notes/issue-*:refs/issue-remotes/<remote>/issue-* (likewise the edge index ref). A fetch never touches local issue refs.
 2. Integrate per issue, after fetch and in post-merge: for each issue whose local and tracking refs differ, fast-forward when one contains the other; otherwise git merge-base, a field-level three-way merge of the issue document (comments as a union by timestamp and author), and a commit with both parents. A field changed on both sides is a conflict: report it, keep both values recorded, and leave the issue for the user to resolve (git issue resolve <id>), never drop one. Unchanged and fast-forward issues cost one sha comparison, so speed is kept.
 3. Push without force: the pre-push hook pushes only changed refs (as now) without +, so a diverged issue ref is rejected like a diverged branch, and the hook says to fetch and integrate first.
 4. Rebuild the edge index incrementally from the merged headers, as reads already do.
 
 Acceptance: the two-clone repro keeps both comments after fetch and after pull; a both-sides status change is reported as a conflict, with neither value lost; a diverged push is rejected; git issue list and the pre-push hook stay O(changed refs) in git calls.

